RxjsOperators
An operator allows us to process the values emitted by Observables.Operators generally create a new Observable rather than modifying the original one.
*Map()
map() transforms each emitted value into another value.
const numbers$ = of(1, 2, 3);
const doubled$ = numbers$.pipe(
  map(x => x * 2)
);

*filter() 
filter() allows values that satisfy a condition and ignores values that don't.

const numbers$ = of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

const result$ = numbers$.pipe(
  filter(x => x % 2 === 0)
);

*tap()
tap() is used to perform a side effect without changing the emitted value.
tap() observes the value but doesn't transform it.

That's why it is commonly used for:

logging
debugging
metrics
triggering some side effect
But not for transforming the stream.

*scan()
scan() maintains an accumulator and emits the accumulated result after each source emission.
of(1, 2, 3).pipe(
  scan((acc, value) => acc + value, 0)
)

*take()
take() is a filtering-style operator that limits how many values we receive.
take(n) emits the first n values from the source Observable and then completes the resulting Observable.

*takeUntill()
takeUntil(notifier$) keeps emitting source values until the notifier Observable emits. Then the resulting Observable completes.
source$.pipe(
  takeUntil(stop$)
)
The source emits until stop$ emits something.

take() vs takeUntil()
Very important:

take(3)
→ stop after 3 values


takeUntil(stop$)
→ stop when stop$ emits

So:

take()
→ count-based stopping

takeUntil()
→ signal-based stopping

*distinctUntilChanged()
Its job is to prevent consecutive duplicate values.
distinctUntilChanged() removes consecutive duplicate values, not all duplicates.

*debounceTime()
debounceTime(duration) waits for a period of silence before emitting the latest value. Every new value resets the timer.
source$.pipe(
  debounceTime(500)
)
Means:
Wait for 500ms of silence before emitting the latest value.

*throttleTime()
throttleTime(duration) limits how frequently values are emitted. After a value is emitted, subsequent values are suppressed during the throttle period.


Higher Order Observables

Observable that emits another Observable are called Higher Order Observables
We don't usually want the subscriber to receive Observable objects.So flattening is required before theobservable is  subscribed
RxJS provide  following  flattening  operators

*switchMap()
switchMap() unsubscribes from the previous inner Observable when a new outer value arrives.
Why is switchMap() useful?
The classic example is search/autocomplete.

Imagine the user types:

a
an
ang
angu
angul
angula
angular

Each input could trigger an HTTP request.

We don't necessarily care about the results for old searches.

If the user has now typed:

angular

we primarily want the result for the latest search.

So:

searchControl.valueChanges.pipe(
  debounceTime(300),
  switchMap(term => searchApi(term))
)

Conceptually:

"ang"
 ↓
request A ─────────X

"angu"
 ↓
request B ─────────X

"angular"
 ↓
request C ───────────────→ result

The previous inner subscriptions are cancelled when newer searches arrive.

*mergeMap()
mergeMap() subscribes to each inner Observable and allows them to run concurrently.
Nothing gets cancelled simply because a new outer value arrived.

For example:

Inner A: A1 → A2 → A3
Inner B: B1 → B2 → B3

The resulting Observable might emit:

A1
B1
A2
B2
A3
B3

The exact order depends on the timing of the inner Observables.

*concatMap()
concatMap() processes inner Observables one at a time, in order.
Unlike mergeMap(), it doesn't run them concurrently.

mergeMap() vs concatMap() ⭐
Very important:

mergeMap()
A starts
B starts
C starts

→ concurrent

concatMap()
A starts
B waits
C waits

A completes
 ↓
B starts

B completes
 ↓
C starts

→ sequential

So put this in your notes:

mergeMap()
→ concurrent

concatMap()
→ sequential + ordered

exhaustMap()
exhaustMap() ignores new outer values while the current inner Observable is running.
Real-world example ⭐
A classic use case is a Submit button.

Imagine the user clicks:

Submit
Submit
Submit

The first click starts an HTTP request:

Submit
  ↓
HTTP request
  ↓
running...

If the user clicks again while that request is still running:

Submit → IGNORE
Submit → IGNORE

You don't necessarily want three identical requests.

So:

submitClicks$.pipe(
  exhaustMap(() => saveData$())
)

means:

"Start the first save. Ignore additional clicks until that save finishes."
mergeMap(value => inner$(value), 2)

only 2 at a time.

Notes ✍️
mergeMap() supports a concurrency limit. When the limit is reached, additional inner Observables wait until an active inner Observable completes.

Error  Handling  Operators

*catchError()
catchError() catches an error from an Observable and replaces the failed stream with another Observable.
catchError() can either:
recover with a replacement Observable
rethrow the error after doing something with it

*throwError()
throwError() is a creation function that creates an Observable which immediately sends an error notification.
Why would we use it?
A common use is inside catchError() when we want to rethrow the error.

source$.pipe(
  catchError(error => {
    console.log('Logging:', error);

    return throwError(() => error);
  })
)

Flow:

source
  ↓
error
  ↓
catchError
  ↓
log error
  ↓
throwError
  ↓
error reaches subscriber

*finalize()
finalize() executes a callback when the Observable terminates or the subscription is unsubscribed.
finalize()
→ runs on complete
→ runs on error
→ runs on unsubscribe
→ commonly used for cleanup/loading state

*retyr()
This is used when an Observable fails and we want to subscribe to the source again.
apiCall$.pipe(
  retry(2),
  catchError(() => of('Fallback'))
)
Suppose the API fails every time.

Attempt 1 → error
     ↓
Attempt 2 → error
     ↓
Attempt 3 → error
     ↓
catchError()
     ↓
"Fallback"
     ↓
complete

Notice the order:

retry()
  ↓
catchError()

retry() gets the opportunity to retry first.

Only after the retries are exhausted does the error reach catchError().

But placement matters ⭐
Consider:

source$.pipe(
  catchError(() => of('Fallback')),
  retry(2)
)

Now catchError() handles the error before retry() sees it.

It replaces the error with:

of('Fallback')

That replacement Observable completes normally.

Therefore, there may be nothing for retry() to retry.

*startWith()
allows you to specify values that should be emitted before the source Observable starts emitting its own values.
of(3).pipe(
  startWith(1, 2)
)

Output:

1
2
3
complete

*endWith()
It appends values after the source completes normally.
of(10, 20).pipe(
  endWith(30, 40)
)

Output:

10
20
30
40
complete

The source must finish before 30 and 40 are emitted.

In case of errors or if we unsubscribe endsWith() will not be callled

*defaultIfEmpty() 
emits a default value if the source completes without emitting anything.

*first()
It emits the first value and then completes.If no matching value exists and no default is supplied, it errors when the source completes.

Example:

of(10, 20, 30).pipe(
  first()
)

Output:

10
complete

It doesn't wait for 20 or 30.
This is similar to:

take(1)

because:

source$.pipe(
  take(1)
)

also takes the first value and completes.

So why have first()?
first() has some additional behavior.

You can provide a predicate.

For example:

of(10, 20, 30, 40).pipe(
  first(x => x > 20)
)

It looks for the first value satisfying the condition.

Check each value:

10 → x > 20? ❌

20 → x > 20? ❌
30 → x > 20? ✅

Therefore:

30
complete

*last()
last() waits for the source to complete and then emits the last value

*find()
find() emits the first value that satisfies the predicate.find() is similar to first() with a predicate,
but there is an important difference in what it returns.
What happens when nothing matches?

With find():

of(10, 20).pipe(
  find(x => x > 100)
)

There is no match.

find() emits:

undefined
complete

It does not throw an error.

Compare:

first(predicate)
→ no match → ERROR ❌

find(predicate)
→ no match → undefined

*findIndex()
similar to find(), but instead of returning the matching value, it returns the matching index.

No value matches.

findIndex() emits:

-1
complete

---CombinationOperators---

*combineLatest()
combineLatest() combines multiple Observables and emits whenever any one of them emits, using the latest value from every Observable.
Important rule ⭐
combineLatest() doesn't emit until every source has emitted at least once.

*withLatestFrom()
withLatestFrom() combines the latest value from one or more other Observables with the value emitted by the source Observable.
combineLatest() vs withLatestFrom() ⭐
combineLatest()	withLatestFrom()
Any Observable can trigger emission	Only source Observable triggers emission
Emits latest values from all sources	Emits source value + latest other values
Useful when any change matters	Useful when a specific event triggers the calculation

*zip()
zip() combines multiple Observables by pairing their values according to their emission order. It waits until each Observable has emitted the corresponding value and then emits those values together.








